{"maxId":323,"entries":{"chapter-96-study-coagulation-disorders|Chapter 96 Study: Coagulation Disorders":["u1"],"summary|Summary":["u2"],"copy|Hemostasis progresses from platelet plug formation through coagulation, endogenous anticoagulant regulation and fibrinolysis (p1509–1510).":["u3"],"copy|Aging increases both bleeding and venous thrombosis risk. Acquired disorders and platelet number/function abnormalities are important causes of bleeding (p1509–1510).":["u4"],"copy|Annual VTE incidence is approximately 2–3 per 1000 overall and 7 per 1000 after 70 (p1515).":["u5"],"copy|Clinical findings alone do not establish DVT: combine pretest probability, laboratory testing and imaging. Wells is the best-validated and most-used DVT prediction model (p1517).":["u6"],"copy|D-dimer also rises with age, infection, inflammation, cancer and surgery; false positives reduce specificity in older adults (p1517).":["u7"],"copy|PE may present nonspecifically with dyspnea, tachypnea, tachycardia or chest pain. Symptoms help estimate probability but cannot confirm the diagnosis (p1518).":["u8"],"copy|CTPA is the primary imaging test for suspected PE unless contraindicated, including severe renal insufficiency or contrast allergy (p1519).":["u9"],"copy|For newly diagnosed primary ITP within 3 months, platelets below 30 × 10^9/L and no or minor mucocutaneous bleeding: prednisone 0.5–2.0 mg/kg/day or dexamethasone 40 mg/day for 4 days (p1514).":["u10"],"copy|With corticosteroids, monitor particularly for hypertension/hyperglycemia and insomnia, depression, gastric irritation, myopathy or osteoporosis (p1514).":["u11"],"copy|Anticoagulants reduce VTE recurrence and mortality; choose treatment and duration by balancing recurrence against bleeding risk (p1515).":["u12"],"copy|Warfarin requires parenteral anticoagulant overlap for at least 5 days and until INR is above 2.0 for at least 24 hours; therapeutic INR is 2.0–3.0 (p1520).":["u13"],"copy|Warfarin requires close INR monitoring because older adults may be more sensitive and have more food/drug interactions. DOACs are effective in carefully selected older adults (p1509, p1520).":["u14"],"copy|Uncomplicated DVT can generally be treated at home, but comorbid illness, bleeding risk or inadequate financial/home support may warrant admission (p1519).":["u15"],"copy|More sensitive CT detection of subsegmental PE has not been accompanied by a corresponding mortality decline; detection does not alone establish clinical significance (p1519).":["u16"],"exam-cited-book-images|Exam-cited book images":["u17"],"figure-96-3-book-image|Figure 96-3 — book image":["u18"],"copy|Hazzard 8e, Figure 96-3, p.1514":["u19","u37"],"table-96-2-book-image|Table 96-2 — book image":["u20"],"copy|Hazzard 8e, Table 96-2, p.1513":["u21","u38"],"exam-questions|Exam questions":["u22"],"copy|2025-Jun Q99":["u25"],"copy|CHAPTER 96s / HAZZARD":["u26"],"copy|Study summary of Chapter 96 - Coagulation Disorders; the book and the full chapter are the reference.":["u27"],"copy|Hemostasis & risk":["u28"],"copy|Diagnosis":["u29"],"copy|Management & drugs":["u30"],"copy|Exam traps":["u31"],"copy|Book images cited by past exams.":["u32"],"copy|Tap an image to zoom.":["u33"],"copy|Past-exam questions linked to this chapter (3):":["u36"],"copy|CHAPTER 96 / HAZZARD":["u26"],"copy|PT tests the extrinsic and common pathways and is the most sensitive test for factor VII deficiency; aPTT tests the intrinsic and common pathways and detects deficiencies of, or inhibitors to, factors II, V, VIII, IX, X, XI and XII (p1512).":["u39"],"copy|Normal PT, prolonged aPTT: lupus anticoagulant; factor VIII, IX, XI or XII deficiency; von Willebrand disease; heparin exposure. Subcutaneous heparin prophylaxis is the most common cause in hospitalized patients. Factor XI deficiency (hemophilia C) has a more variable bleeding history, often following surgical procedures. The lupus anticoagulant causes thrombosis rather than bleeding (p1512–1513).":["u40"],"copy|Prolonged PT, normal aPTT: VKA use, dietary vitamin K deficiency, factor VII deficiency, early liver disease, early DIC, high-dose heparin. Both prolonged: supratherapeutic anticoagulation, concomitant heparin and VKA, end-stage liver disease, DIC, systemic fibrinolysis, rodenticide poisoning (Table 96-2, p1513).":["u41"],"copy|Acquired hemophilia A (autoantibodies against factor VIII) is predominantly a disease of older adults, median age 64–78; up to 50% have no identifiable cause. Acute subcutaneous, gastrointestinal or muscle bleeding without trauma or a prior bleeding history should prompt evaluation; mortality exceeds 20% if diagnosis is delayed (p1512).":["u42"],"copy|Causes in older adults: decreased production (B12 or folate deficiency, myelodysplastic syndrome, marrow failure or infiltration, medications, alcohol, infection), increased destruction (immune thrombocytopenia, SLE, antiphospholipid syndrome, HIT, drug-induced thrombocytopenia, infection, DIC, mechanical prosthetic valve, TTP), sequestration or other (chronic liver disease, hypersplenism, spurious, dilutional) (Table 96-3, p1513).":["u43"],"copy|ITP is isolated thrombocytopenia below 100 × 10^9/L; incidence 2 to 5 per 100,000, increasing with age. Petechiae, purpura and epistaxis usually appear below 30 × 10^9/L (p1514).":["u44"],"copy|Drug-induced thrombocytopenia is the most common cause of thrombocytopenia in older adults and is usually evident within the first week of the causative drug. Figure 96-3 sorts it by onset: <24 h, immune-mediated by preexisting antibodies; 3–10 days, immune-mediated anamnestic response; >2 weeks, nonimmune myelosuppression or immune-mediated primary alloimmunization. Table 96-4 lists trimethoprim/sulfamethoxazole among definite or probable causes (p1514).":["u45"],"copy|HIT: antibodies to platelet factor 4 increase platelet activation and consumption and raise the risk of both arterial and venous thrombosis; use validated tools such as the 4T score with serologic or functional testing (p1514–1515).":["u46"],"copy|Virchow triad: endothelial injury, impaired flow (stasis) and hypercoagulability. Factor V Leiden is the most common inherited thrombophilia, prevalence 5% in unselected White populations, with a three- to fivefold VTE risk; prothrombin G20210A gives about a threefold risk (p1516).":["u47"],"copy|Acquired thrombophilias in older adults include antiphospholipid syndrome, HIT, hormone replacement therapy, myeloproliferative neoplasms and malignancy. Antiphospholipid syndrome needs persistent antiphospholipid antibodies with arterial or venous thrombosis or pregnancy complications; antibodies can also cause thrombocytopenia. Repeat an initial positive test after at least 12 weeks; routine screening is not recommended (p1516).":["u48"],"copy|Malignancy carries a four- to sevenfold VTE risk, up to 20% of all VTEs. Some anticancer therapies are prothrombotic (eg, lenalidomide, cisplatin, L-asparaginase). Polycythemia vera and essential thrombocythemia, especially with JAK2 V617F, are associated with arterial, venous and splanchnic thrombosis (p1516).":["u49"],"working-from-the-presentation-to-the-test|Working from the presentation to the test":["u319"],"copy|After excessive bleeding at an operation, ask about dental extraction, previous operations and spontaneous bruising. An isolated prolonged aPTT with a normal PT and platelet count changes the differential. In factor XI deficiency the history can be quiet until a hemostatic challenge. Vitamin K deficiency more often prolongs PT, while advanced DIC can prolong both tests (p1512–1513).":["u320"],"copy|A sudden low platelet count after starting trimethoprim/sulfamethoxazole fits drug-induced thrombocytopenia. A fall at 3–10 days points to an anamnestic immune response in Figure 96-3. This differs from platelet activation in HIT, where a falling count can herald an arterial or venous clot (p1514–1515).":["u321"],"copy|Recurrent ischemic events with otherwise unexplained thrombocytopenia warrant considering antiphospholipid syndrome. Look for persistent antibodies in the appropriate clinical setting. Thrombocytopenia may accompany the syndrome even though thrombosis is its characteristic vascular complication (p1516).":["u67"],"copy|For suspected PE in an outpatient aged 50 years or older with a suitable pretest probability, the book describes an age-adjusted D-dimer cutoff of age × 10 µg/L for assays with a conventional 500 µg/L cutoff. A positive value is nonspecific. V/Q scanning can help when severe renal insufficiency or contrast allergy limits CTPA (p1519).":["u68"],"copy|Before and during anticoagulation, review kidney and liver function, platelet count, concomitant NSAIDs or antiplatelet drugs, and the patient's ability to take treatment reliably. Correctable risks include uncontrolled hypertension and unnecessary dual antithrombotic exposure (p1521–1522).":["u69"],"a-platelet-plug-a-fibrin-mesh-and-the-tests-that-sample-them|A platelet plug, a fibrin mesh and the tests that sample them":["u70"],"bleeding-after-a-challenge-or-bleeding-without-one|Bleeding after a challenge or bleeding without one":["u71"],"a-falling-count-establish-that-it-is-real-then-explain-the-mechanism|A falling count: establish that it is real, then explain the mechanism":["u72"],"separate-an-arterial-thrombotic-syndrome-from-common-causes-of-dvt|Separate an arterial thrombotic syndrome from common causes of DVT":["u73"],"suspected-dvt-use-the-wells-score-before-ordering-the-next-test|Suspected DVT: use the Wells score before ordering the next test":["u74"],"suspected-pe-the-probability-cutoff-and-the-imaging-question-differ|Suspected PE: the probability cutoff and the imaging question differ":["u75"],"anticoagulants-distinguish-immediate-treatment-the-oral-regimen-and-reversal|Anticoagulants: distinguish immediate treatment, the oral regimen and reversal":["u76"],"prevention-in-a-hospitalized-medical-patient|Prevention in a hospitalized medical patient":["u77"],"exam-traps|Exam traps":["u31"],"copy|Distinguish the cutoff equalities: Wells DVT is likely at exactly two points; Wells PE remains unlikely at exactly four (p1517, p1519).":["u79"],"copy|A late presentation after dental work supports factor XI deficiency; spontaneous large hematomas with no prior history raise suspicion for an acquired inhibitor (p1512–1513).":["u80"],"copy|In drug-induced thrombocytopenia the delayed branch includes both nonimmune marrow suppression and primary alloimmunization (p1514).":["u81"],"copy|The Padua score stratifies prophylaxis need in hospitalized medical patients. It cannot confirm acute VTE or quantify bleeding risk (p1522).":["u82"],"past-exam-questions|Past exam questions":["u83"],"recall-drill|Recall drill":["u88"],"copy|Study summary of Chapter 96; the book and the full chapter are the reference.":["u89"],"copy|Prolonged PT or aPTT (Table 96-2)":["u50"],"copy|Thrombocytopenia (Table 96-3, Figure 96-3)":["u51"],"copy|Thrombosis risk (p1516)":["u52"],"copy|Figure 96-1 starts with exposed subendothelium.":["u93"],"copy|Collagen and other exposed structures activate platelets.":["u94"],"copy|Platelet glycoprotein Ib-alpha binds von Willebrand factor and mediates adhesion.":["u95"],"copy|Glycoprotein IIb/IIIa binds fibrinogen and helps platelets aggregate.":["u96"],"copy|In the second panel, monocytes and other cells are incorporated and fibrin strands reinforce the plug.":["u97"],"copy|This sequence explains why petechiae and nose or gum bleeding suggest a platelet or vessel problem, while a palpable soft-tissue hematoma or prolonged bleeding at surgery raises suspicion for a coagulation defect.":["u98"],"copy|Neither presentation by itself establishes the diagnosis (p1509–1511).":["u99"],"copy|Figure 96-2 can be read two ways.":["u100"],"copy|For interpreting PT and aPTT, trace the extrinsic tissue factor–VIIa route or the intrinsic XII–XI–IX route to factor X.":["u101"],"copy|With activated factor V, Xa supports conversion of prothrombin to thrombin.":["u102"],"copy|Thrombin generates fibrin from fibrinogen, and XIIIa stabilizes the mesh by crosslinking it.":["u103"],"copy|For understanding what happens in the vessel, follow Table 96-1.":["u104"],"copy|Initiation at tissue-factor-bearing surfaces produces the first small amount of thrombin.":["u105"],"copy|During amplification that thrombin activates platelets and factors V, VIII and XI.":["u106"],"copy|Propagation on the activated platelet brings tenase and prothrombinase together, producing the burst of thrombin needed for a stable plug.":["u107"],"copy|During termination, antithrombin, tissue factor pathway inhibitor and proteins C and S restrict clotting to the injured area.":["u108"],"copy|Plasmin subsequently breaks down fibrin.":["u109"],"copy|D-dimer is a product of breakdown of crosslinked fibrin, which explains why it signals turnover but does not locate a clot (p1509–1512, p1517).":["u110"],"copy|Aging affects several parts of this system simultaneously.":["u111"],"copy|In healthy subjects fibrinogen rises by approximately 10 mg/dL per decade and von Willebrand factor by about 10% to 20% per decade.":["u112"],"copy|More plasminogen activator inhibitor limits fibrinolysis, while higher plasmin-antiplasmin complexes and D-dimer also provide evidence of increased fibrin turnover.":["u113"],"copy|The net clinical question still depends on the patient.":["u114"],"copy|An older adult taking several antiplatelet agents may bleed despite having risk factors for venous thrombosis (p1510–1511, p1515).":["u115"],"copy|Construct a timeline before interpreting a long clotting time.":["u116"],"copy|Ask about operations and tooth extractions, whether bleeding was immediate or prolonged, past transfusion or iron deficiency, heavy menses or postpartum bleeding, and relatives with a similar history.":["u117"],"copy|An inherited defect may first become clinically apparent late in life if the patient has rarely faced a hemostatic challenge.":["u118"],"copy|A patient who previously tolerated surgery but now has extensive spontaneous bruising needs a different investigation.":["u119"],"copy|Record prescribed medicines, herbal and over-the-counter agents explicitly.":["u120"],"copy|A recent change in diet also matters when considering vitamin K deficiency (p1511–1512).":["u121"],"copy|June 2023 Q46 illustrates the first pattern.":["u122"],"copy|After prostate surgery the patient has prolonged urinary bleeding.":["u123"],"copy|Previous dental extraction had also caused bleeding, but spontaneous events were absent.":["u124"],"copy|PT and platelet count are normal and aPTT is prolonged.":["u125"],"copy|Locate that combination in Table 96-2 before choosing among diagnoses.":["u126"],"copy|Factor XI deficiency can give this variable, procedure-associated presentation.":["u127"],"copy|A vitamin K antagonist or isolated factor VII deficiency better explains a long PT.":["u128"],"copy|A lupus anticoagulant can lengthen aPTT, but the history would more typically point to thrombosis.":["u129"],"copy|In a hospital patient also review heparin exposure before pursuing a rare deficiency.":["u130"],"copy|The test pattern narrows the possibilities; the history ranks them (p1512–1513).":["u131"],"copy|By contrast, spontaneous new subcutaneous, gastrointestinal or muscular bleeding in an older person without a personal or family history can signal an acquired factor VIII inhibitor.":["u132"],"copy|The disorder can accompany another illness or appear without an identifiable cause.":["u133"],"copy|Delayed recognition can be fatal.":["u134"],"copy|Check hemoglobin, platelet count, PT and aPTT first, then pursue factor or inhibitor testing with hematology when indicated.":["u135"],"copy|A normal platelet count does not guarantee normal platelet function.":["u136"],"copy|Aspirin, clopidogrel, ticagrelor, dipyridamole and cilostazol may impair hemostasis; nonsteroidals and some other commonly prescribed drugs also affect platelets.":["u137"],"copy|Kidney and liver disease can impair function even in the absence of an absolute platelet deficiency (p1511–1512, p1515).":["u138"],"copy|Read Table 96-3 by asking where platelets went.":["u139"],"copy|An additional cytopenia, a history of heavy alcohol intake or clues to folate or B12 deficiency point toward impaired production.":["u140"],"copy|Myelodysplasia and marrow infiltration deserve attention in an older adult.":["u141"],"copy|Infection can suppress production or accelerate immune clearance.":["u142"],"copy|A mechanical valve, disseminated intravascular coagulation and thrombotic microangiopathy belong on the consumption side.":["u143"],"copy|Hypersplenism and liver disease may sequester platelets; recent massive transfusion or fluid resuscitation can dilute them.":["u144"],"copy|When a low laboratory value is unexpected, inspect the smear for clumps.":["u145"],"copy|EDTA-dependent clumping in the specimen can yield a false low count; repeat sampling in citrate can resolve the discrepancy (p1513–1515).":["u146"],"copy|Primary ITP is a diagnosis of exclusion.":["u147"],"copy|The count may be very low in a patient with surprisingly little bleeding.":["u148"],"copy|Review the CBC, reticulocytes, metabolic profile and peripheral smear, and assess for secondary causes, including HIV and hepatitis C.":["u149"],"copy|The smear may show occasional large platelets.":["u150"],"copy|A marrow examination is more relevant when other unexplained cytopenias accompany the low platelet count.":["u151"],"copy|An isolated low count plus mucocutaneous bleeding fits ITP but must be reconciled with the medication history and timing.":["u152"],"copy|The chapter's initial steroid regimens above apply to newly diagnosed primary ITP with no or minor mucocutaneous bleeding, not a universal emergency regimen for a major hemorrhage (p1513–1515).":["u153"],"copy|June 2026 Q12 combines a drug listed in Table 96-4 with a timing clue in Figure 96-3.":["u154"],"copy|Trimethoprim/sulfamethoxazole can cause immune platelet loss.":["u155"],"copy|After several days of exposure, an abrupt count decline and petechiae fit the anamnestic response branch.":["u156"],"copy|Preexisting antibodies are the explanation the figure gives for onset in under 24 hours.":["u157"],"copy|Marrow suppression and primary alloimmunization fall in the over 2 weeks branch.":["u158"],"copy|The table also implicates drugs as diverse as vancomycin, piperacillin, quinine, phenytoin, furosemide and several glycoprotein IIb/IIIa inhibitors.":["u159"],"copy|Presence on the list supports an exposure history; it does not establish causation for every patient receiving that agent (p1514).":["u160"],"copy|Heparin is the important exception to the intuition that a fall in platelets is primarily a bleeding event.":["u161"],"copy|Antibodies involving platelet factor 4 activate and consume platelets while driving thrombosis.":["u162"],"copy|In a recently operated, septic patient, there may be plausible alternative explanations for thrombocytopenia.":["u163"],"copy|A new DVT occurring with the decline shifts concern toward HIT, the distinction made in the older exam.":["u164"],"copy|Assess the timing and extent of the fall, thrombosis and alternative causes with a clinical prediction tool and interpret antibody or functional tests in that context (p1514–1515).":["u165"],"copy|Table 96-3 places antiphospholipid syndrome among causes of increased platelet destruction.":["u166"],"copy|In June 2026 Basic Q107, recurrent ischemic strokes plus otherwise unexplained persistent thrombocytopenia point toward that syndrome.":["u167"],"copy|The low count matters because thrombosis and thrombocytopenia can coexist.":["u168"],"copy|Palpitations would instead raise the possibility of an arrhythmia and embolism.":["u169"],"copy|History alone cannot establish antiphospholipid syndrome: combine a qualifying clinical history with persistent laboratory findings, repeating initially positive antibody testing at the interval above.":["u170"],"copy|A transient positive test is insufficient (p1513, p1516).":["u171"],"copy|June 2025 Q99 asks for the exception among DVT risks.":["u172"],"copy|Recent hospitalization, impaired mobility, surgery, nephrotic syndrome, malignancy and myeloproliferative disease all provide recognizable pathways to thrombosis.":["u173"],"copy|Review hormone treatment and cancer therapy too.":["u174"],"copy|Escitalopram does not appear among the chapter's established DVT risks.":["u175"],"copy|It belongs to a drug class discussed for potential inhibition of platelet function.":["u176"],"copy|An adverse effect on hemostasis is different from a tendency to form venous thrombi.":["u177"],"copy|Similarly, an inherited variant may increase relative risk substantially without being the most likely precipitant in an older patient recently admitted after hip fracture (p1515–1516).":["u178"],"copy|Table 96-5 awards one point apiece for active cancer, paralysis or immobilization of the leg, specified bed rest or recent surgery, deep venous tenderness, whole-leg swelling, calf asymmetry, asymmetric pitting edema, nonvaricose collaterals and previous DVT.":["u179"],"copy|The calf criterion is a difference over 3 cm measured 10 cm below the tibial tuberosity.":["u180"],"copy|The table's history criterion specifies bed rest longer than three days or major surgery within four weeks.":["u181"],"copy|Subtract 2 points if another diagnosis is at least as likely.":["u182"],"copy|A total below 2 is unlikely; 2 or more is likely.":["u183"],"copy|The competing-diagnosis item is a judgment, so an obvious competing explanation can change the testing sequence even with a swollen leg (p1517).":["u184"],"copy|Figure 96-4 begins the unlikely pathway with D-dimer.":["u185"],"copy|If negative, stop the DVT workup in this pathway; if positive, obtain compression ultrasound.":["u186"],"copy|On the likely pathway begin with ultrasound.":["u187"],"copy|Failure of a deep vein to compress supports thrombosis.":["u188"],"copy|A negative initial ultrasound in a likely case requires attention to how much of the leg was imaged.":["u189"],"copy|The figure's footnote calls for D-dimer; if positive, repeat compression ultrasound in 1 week, especially after an initial study confined to the proximal veins.":["u190"],"copy|The attraction of a highly sensitive D-dimer assay lies in its negative result at low probability.":["u191"],"copy|An elevated result in an older postoperative patient does not distinguish a new clot from inflammation or recovery (p1517–1518).":["u192"],"copy|In Table 96-6, signs of DVT and the judgment that PE is the most likely diagnosis each earn 3 points.":["u193"],"copy|A pulse over 100/min, immobilization or surgery in the previous four weeks, and previous VTE each earn 1.5.":["u194"],"copy|Hemoptysis and active cancer each earn 1.":["u195"],"copy|In this two-level model a score over 4 makes PE likely; exactly four remains in the unlikely group.":["u196"],"copy|A recent hip operation, tachycardia and hemoptysis should raise suspicion even if the person is normotensive.":["u197"],"copy|The Wells model estimates the probability of PE.":["u198"],"copy|PESI and simplified PESI instead estimate short-term mortality in someone with PE and help inform whether outpatient treatment is feasible (p1518–1519).":["u199"],"copy|A chest radiograph helps identify pneumonia or pneumothorax.":["u200"],"copy|It is often normal in acute PE.":["u201"],"copy|A pleural-based wedge may suggest infarction, and focal oligemia can mark decreased perfusion, but neither is required.":["u202"],"copy|CTPA can assess right ventricular dilation and detect alternative causes of symptoms.":["u203"],"copy|When contrast cannot be given, interpret the V/Q result alongside the chest film and pretest probability.":["u204"],"copy|In the evidence cited, a high clinical probability paired with a high-probability scan gave a 96% likelihood of PE; low clinical and low scan probability together gave 4%.":["u205"],"copy|A normal perfusion scan essentially excluded PE, whereas a nondiagnostic scan requires another step (p1519).":["u206"],"copy|Persistent symptoms change the differential after treatment.":["u207"],"copy|In 20%–50% of people with DVT, post-thrombotic syndrome develops over the following 1–2 years.":["u208"],"copy|Residual obstruction and damaged venous valves can lead to heaviness, edema, induration, hyperpigmentation and sometimes ulceration.":["u209"],"copy|Chronic dyspnea or fatigue after PE can indicate chronic thromboembolic pulmonary hypertension, which the chapter reports in 0.4%–6.2% of patients.":["u210"],"copy|These are follow-up complications with different mechanisms, not in themselves proof of a new acute embolus (p1517).":["u211"],"copy|UFH has a role when renal impairment or bleeding risk favors an agent that can be stopped and rapidly reversed.":["u212"],"copy|The chapter's typical intravenous regimen begins with 80 U/kg followed by 18 U/kg/h, with aPTT or anti-Xa checked every 6 hours until stable.":["u213"],"copy|Its cited anti-Xa target is 0.35–0.7 U/mL.":["u214"],"copy|A less common subcutaneous regimen in selected patients is 333 U/kg followed by 250 U/kg every 12 hours.":["u215"],"copy|LMWH has more predictable activity at a weight-based dose and can support outpatient management in selected patients.":["u216"],"copy|For uncomplicated DVT, confirm the person can obtain and use the medicine and has adequate support before relying on home treatment (p1519–1520).":["u217"],"copy|Table 96-7 gives the following acute VTE regimens.":["u218"],"copy|Apixaban starts at 10 mg twice daily for 7 days, then 5 mg twice daily.":["u219"],"copy|Rivaroxaban starts at 15 mg twice daily for 21 days, then 20 mg daily.":["u220"],"copy|Both provide immediate treatment without a heparin lead-in.":["u221"],"copy|The table lists 150 mg twice daily for dabigatran, a direct thrombin inhibitor, after 5 days of parenteral anticoagulation.":["u222"],"copy|Edoxaban, like apixaban and rivaroxaban, inhibits Xa, but it also follows a heparin lead-in.":["u223"],"copy|The book gives 60 mg daily, reduced to 30 mg daily at creatinine clearance 15–50 mL/min; avoid below 15.":["u224"],"copy|Its renal column says to avoid rivaroxaban and dabigatran below 30 mL/min and gives no renal adjustment for the apixaban treatment regimen (p1520).":["u225"],"copy|Current practice (not the book): U.S. rivaroxaban labeling gives the DVT/PE regimen with food at creatinine clearance of 15 mL/min or higher.":["u226"],"copy|Patients below 30 mL/min were excluded from treatment trials and need close observation for bleeding.":["u227"],"copy|For adult DVT/PE, dabigatran labeling requires clearance above 30 mL/min and 5–10 days of parenteral treatment.":["u228"],"copy|Edoxaban labeling also reduces the VTE dose at body weight ≤60 kg or with certain P-glycoprotein inhibitors. - FDA rivaroxaban label, 2026, FDA dabigatran label, 2026, FDA edoxaban label, 2023.":["u229"],"copy|Systemic thrombolysis may be warranted when acute PE compromises circulation.":["u230"],"copy|The book defines this as systolic pressure below 90 mm Hg or a drop of at least 40 mm Hg from baseline.":["u231"],"copy|Right ventricular dysfunction without that compromise is a different setting.":["u232"],"copy|In PEITHO, less hemodynamic deterioration came at a cost of major bleeding and hemorrhagic stroke, without a mortality benefit.":["u233"],"copy|Likewise, adding catheter-directed treatment to anticoagulation for proximal DVT did not lower overall post-thrombotic syndrome in ATTRACT and increased early major bleeding.":["u234"],"copy|Distinguish preventing circulatory collapse today from trying to prevent a possible chronic complication (p1520–1521).":["u235"],"copy|A transient provocation such as surgery or trauma usually supports a 3-month course in the chapter.":["u236"],"copy|For extended therapy revisit the benefit and bleeding risk periodically, especially as renal function, other prescriptions or independence change.":["u238"],"copy|Table 96-8 gives HAS-BLED components: uncontrolled hypertension, liver or kidney dysfunction, stroke, bleeding, labile INR, age and drugs or alcohol.":["u239"],"copy|Two points are possible if both organs are affected or both exposure categories apply.":["u240"],"copy|Its hypertension cutoff is systolic pressure over 160 mm Hg, and its labile-INR criterion is time in range under 60%.":["u241"],"copy|Use Table 96-9 to turn a score into action: reassess an unnecessary antiplatelet, replace an NSAID when possible, address blood pressure and falls, review prior GI bleeding, and arrange follow-up when INR control or adherence is poor (p1521–1522).":["u242"],"copy|For urgent reversal, protamine reverses UFH and only partly reverses LMWH.":["u243"],"copy|Vitamin K reverses warfarin over about 24 hours, whereas four-factor prothrombin complex concentrate supplies factors promptly when immediate reversal is needed.":["u244"],"copy|Idarucizumab binds dabigatran and was introduced for uncontrolled or life-threatening bleeding or urgent procedures.":["u245"],"copy|The chapter describes andexanet alfa as an approved factor Xa decoy for life-threatening or uncontrolled bleeding with rivaroxaban or apixaban and notes uncertainty about clinical benefit (p1522).":["u246"],"copy|Current practice (not the book): The FDA concluded that serious risks outweighed the benefits of Andexxa, and U.S. commercial sales ended in December 2025. - FDA safety communication, 2025.":["u247"],"copy|Table 96-10 gives 3 points each for active malignancy, prior VTE, immobilization for at least three days or known thrombophilia.":["u248"],"copy|Recent surgery or trauma adds 2.":["u249"],"copy|Age at least 70, cardiac or respiratory failure, acute ischemic stroke or infarction, acute infection or rheumatic disease, BMI over 30 and active hormone treatment each add 1.":["u250"],"copy|A total of 4 or more marks high VTE risk.":["u251"],"copy|The older Padua question turns on the combination of active cancer, recent surgery and age; age alone contributes relatively little.":["u252"],"copy|This score addresses prophylaxis in a medical inpatient, not diagnosis of a swollen leg (p1522).":["u253"],"copy|Consider bleeding risk independently.":["u254"],"copy|Active peptic ulceration, platelets below 50 k/µL or age over 85 may weigh against pharmacologic prophylaxis in the chapter.":["u255"],"copy|Intermittent pneumatic compression or graduated stockings can be used when bleeding risk precludes medication, and mechanical measures can accompany drug prophylaxis in selected high-risk surgical settings.":["u256"],"copy|Choice of a regimen still depends on the indication: the chapter lists low-dose UFH, LMWH, fondaparinux and certain oral agents for specific prophylactic settings rather than assigning a single preventive drug to every positive Padua score (p1522).":["u257"],"copy|Earlier sittings, chapter-level mapping — 2022-Jun Q6, 2021-Dec Q25, 2021-Dec Q62.":["u258"],"copy|1.":["u259"],"copy|Which laboratory pattern suggests an intrinsic-pathway problem?":["u260"],"copy|2.":["u261"],"copy|Which factor deficiency can first present with excessive surgical or dental bleeding?":["u262"],"copy|3.":["u263"],"copy|What isolated PT abnormality can arise with inadequate nutrition?":["u264"],"copy|4.":["u265"],"copy|Why can a lupus anticoagulant confound the interpretation of a prolonged aPTT?":["u266"],"copy|5.":["u267"],"copy|What warrants evaluating for acquired factor VIII inhibition?":["u268"],"copy|6.":["u269"],"copy|How does drug-induced thrombocytopenia within hours differ from a fall several days later?":["u270"],"copy|7.":["u271"],"copy|Which complication should be sought when platelets fall during heparin therapy?":["u272"],"copy|8.":["u273"],"copy|When does the book discuss initial corticosteroids for primary ITP?":["u274"],"copy|9.":["u275"],"copy|What do recurrent strokes with thrombocytopenia suggest?":["u276"],"copy|10.":["u277"],"copy|Which conditions in a thrombosis history imply an acquired hypercoagulable state?":["u278"],"copy|11.":["u279"],"copy|How can age complicate D-dimer interpretation?":["u280"],"copy|12.":["u281"],"copy|What age-adjusted D-dimer threshold does the book describe for suspected PE?":["u282"],"copy|13.":["u283"],"copy|When might V/Q scanning help evaluate suspected PE?":["u284"],"copy|14.":["u285"],"copy|What overlap is required when warfarin starts for acute VTE?":["u286"],"copy|15.":["u287"],"copy|What medication changes may reduce bleeding during anticoagulation?":["u288"],"copy|Answer":["u289","u291","u293","u295","u297","u299","u301","u303","u305","u307","u309","u311","u313","u315","u317"],"copy|A prolonged aPTT with normal PT. Consider heparin exposure, factor deficiency or a circulating inhibitor, then use the bleeding history to narrow the possibilities. (p1512–1513)":["u290"],"copy|Factor XI deficiency can cause a variable bleeding history with few spontaneous events. (p1512–1513)":["u292"],"copy|Vitamin K deficiency can prolong PT before the aPTT becomes abnormal. (p1512–1513)":["u294"],"copy|It prolongs a laboratory clotting time while clinically being associated with thrombosis. (p1512–1513)":["u296"],"copy|New spontaneous skin, muscle or GI bleeding in an older adult without a prior bleeding history, particularly with a prolonged aPTT. (p1512)":["u298"],"copy|Preexisting antibodies can produce a fall within 24 hours. An anamnestic immune response typically occurs at 3–10 days in Figure 96-3. (p1514)":["u300"],"copy|Arterial or venous thrombosis. Use the clinical 4T score and appropriate laboratory testing to assess HIT. (p1514–1515)":["u302"],"copy|For newly diagnosed primary ITP, platelets below 30 × 10⁹/L and no or minor mucocutaneous bleeding. Monitor glucose, blood pressure and other steroid toxicity. (p1514)":["u304"],"copy|Consider antiphospholipid syndrome. An initial positive antibody test should be repeated at least 12 weeks later to establish persistence. (p1516)":["u306"],"copy|Examples include malignancy, nephrotic syndrome, antiphospholipid syndrome, HIT and myeloproliferative neoplasms. (p1515–1516)":["u308"],"copy|Aging, acute inflammation, surgery and malignancy can elevate D-dimer without an acute VTE, reducing specificity. (p1517)":["u310"],"copy|Age in years × 10 µg/L in outpatients aged 50 or over when the assay uses a conventional cutoff of 500 µg/L, interpreted with clinical probability. (p1519)":["u312"],"copy|When contrast allergy or severe renal insufficiency makes CTPA unsuitable. A normal chest radiograph makes the scan easier to interpret. (p1519)":["u314"],"copy|Continue parenteral anticoagulation for at least 5 days and until the INR has been above 2.0 for at least 24 hours. (p1520)":["u316"],"copy|Review the need for antiplatelet therapy and NSAIDs. Remove unnecessary antiplatelet treatment and consider an alternative analgesic where appropriate. (p1522)":["u318"],"copy|After unprovoked VTE, recurrence approached 20% at 2 years and 30% at 8 years in that cohort.":["u237"],"copy|June 2026 Q12 — Printed page 1514.":["u84"],"copy|June 2026 Basic Q107 — Printed page 1513.":["u85"],"copy|June 2025 Q99 — Printed page 1516.":["u86"],"copy|June 2023 Q46 — Printed page 1513.":["u87"],"copy|June 2026 Q12":["u322"],"copy|June 2023 Q46":["u323"]}}
